Step 1. The essential extension: Playwright Test for VS Code

This is the single most important addition today:
1. Open Extensions (Ctrl+Shift+X)
2. Search "Playwright Test for VSCode" (by Microsoft) → Install

Step 2. Verify it's working:

1. Give command "npx playwright install" in the terminal.
2. Open your playwright-framework folder (from Day 13) in VS Code
3. Click the Testing icon in the left sidebar (flask icon)
4. You should see tests/example.spec.ts listed with its individual test cases
5. Click the ▶️ next to one test — it should run just that test and show a green checkmark or red X

Step 3. Recommended supporting extensions (quick installs):

1. ESLint — flags code issues/style problems as you type
2. Error Lens — shows TypeScript errors inline on the same line, instead of just underlining (huge time-saver, you'll want this immediately)
3. Prettier — auto-formats your code consistently (optional, but nice for clean commits)

Step 4. Key VS Code settings worth turning on:

Open Settings (Ctrl+,) and search for these:
1. Format On Save → enable it (auto-cleans up formatting every time you save)
2. Editor: Default Formatter → set to Prettier if installed

Step 5. Debugging setup — running a single test in debug mode:

1. Click the ▶️ dropdown next to a test in the Testing sidebar → choose "Debug Test"
2. This opens the browser in headed mode, pauses at breakpoints you set (click in the gutter left of a line number to set one), and lets you inspect variables live.

Step 6. IntelliSense check — confirm Playwright's types are working:

Open tests/example.spec.ts and type page. inside a test block — you should see a dropdown of every available Playwright method (click, fill, goto, etc.) with descriptions. This confirms TypeScript is correctly picking up Playwright's type definitions.